Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:57:48 +0800
Subject: rewind: store snapshots in a content-addressed object store

- Adopts the storage pattern opencode uses (git object database): each
  unique file content is stored once under objects/<sha256> and every
  snapshot's files/ entries are hardlinks into the store, so identical
  content across turns is never duplicated and each snapshot is
  self-contained for restore.
- Unchanged files are linked from the previous snapshot without being
  re-read; objects and manifests are published atomically through
  temporary files, and pruning now garbage-collects objects no longer
  referenced by any retained manifest.
- A best-effort per-workspace lock (PID-based, with stale takeover)
  prevents concurrent codEx instances from colliding on snapshot indices.
- Restored files get their original mtime back so the size+mtime fast
  path keeps working after a restore; manifests written by the previous
  copy-based engine remain readable and restorable through the chain.
- Adds regression tests for content dedup, object garbage collection,
  mtime-preserving restore, legacy-manifest restore, and the lock.
